title: Event log
kind: overview
aliases: log history event-log
summary: Every write is recorded as operations in a sequence kept beside the data, grouped by the labels of the work they belong to — so any change can be audited, undone, or reverted onto the current state.

Every change to a repository's metadata is recorded in its ''event log'', in the same
store and the same transaction as the change itself. The log is a ''sequence'' of
operations, each recording what it replaced, which is what makes it more than an audit
trail:

* ''auditing'' — the full history of any metarecord, and of any piece of work: operations are grouped by nested ''labels'' — a command, a script's run, a context you open ([[Reading the log]], [[Labels and contexts]]);
* ''undo'' and ''redo'' — take back a label of the newest change ''you'' made, and take that back in turn ([[Undo and redo]]);
* ''revert'' — undo a past label or operation by writing what it replaced, onto the current state, as a new write.

Nothing moves back over the log: it only grows, and loses history only by an explicit
prune, or by retention (the last 50 000 operations of the watcher and million of the
rest, by default — [[Automatic retention]]). It stopped being a tree with a movable HEAD,
and its revisions became labels, in October 2026 ([[The operation log]], [[Why the log stops being a tree]]).

